  <h2>19. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <h3>Q 1: How does Cursor IDE know when to use the Ollagraph MCP tool?</h3>

  <p>
    Cursor's agent continuously evaluates prompt context. When your request references external documentation, recent software libraries, or explicit search commands such as <code>/search</code>, the LLM selects the registered <code>search</code> tool dynamically based on its registered JSON Schema.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 2: What is the cost of connecting Cursor to Ollagraph MCP?</h3>

  <p>
    The MCP server itself is free and open source. Each tool call is billed in credits at the cost of the endpoint behind it, and costs vary by endpoint—check the pricing page, or call <code>POST /v1/me/cost-estimate</code> for a specific endpoint. If a call fails, its credits are refunded automatically.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 3: Does Ollagraph store my scraped code or documentation searches?</h3>

  <p>
    No. Ollagraph adheres to a strict zero persistent data retention architecture. Scraped web pages and extracted text are processed in volatile memory and returned directly to Cursor over encrypted TLS 1.3 channels. No content is stored on disk or used for AI model training.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 4: Can I use Ollagraph MCP on Windows, macOS, and Linux?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes. The <code>ollagraph-mcp</code> server is a Python package (Python 3.10+), so it runs anywhere Python and <code>pipx</code> do, including macOS (Intel/Apple Silicon), Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora), and Windows (native PowerShell or WSL2).
  </p>

  <h3>Q 5: What is the difference between stdio and SSE transport modes?</h3>

  <p>
    stdio (Standard Input/Output) launches <code>ollagraph-mcp</code> as a local subprocess directly managed by Cursor IDE. SSE (Server-Sent Events) and the newer HTTP transports connect a client to a remote MCP server over HTTP. Ollagraph does not host a remote MCP endpoint, so stdio is how you connect.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 6: What happens if a web page requires client-side JavaScript rendering?</h3>

  <p>
    Ollagraph automatically detects target page complexity. If a site requires client-side JavaScript execution, Ollagraph routes the request through stealth headless Chromium browser pools—executing JS and returning clean Markdown without additional configuration.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 7: Can Ollagraph parse PDF documentation files directly into Cursor?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes, for PDFs that have a text layer. The <code>convert_pdf_to_markdown</code> tool takes the file as base64-encoded bytes (<code>pdf_base64</code>, plus an optional <code>max_pages</code>), not a URL, and returns Markdown with one section per page. There is no OCR: scanned, image-only PDFs come back flagged with <code>likely_scanned_pdf: true</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 8: How do I update the ollagraph-mcp package to the latest version?</h3>

  <p>
    <code>pipx run ollagraph-mcp</code> uses a cached copy that pipx refreshes periodically. To force the latest release, run <code>pipx run --no-cache ollagraph-mcp</code> once. If you installed it with pip instead, run <code>pip install -U ollagraph-mcp</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 9: Can I run Ollagraph MCP in isolated, air-gapped private cloud VPCs?</h3>

  <p>
    No. Ollagraph is a hosted API at <code>api.ollagraph.com</code>; there is no private-VPC or self-hosted deployment. The MCP server runs locally, but every tool call goes to the hosted API, so the host needs outbound HTTPS access to <code>api.ollagraph.com</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Q 10: How do enterprise engineering managers manage monthly credit quotas across developer teams?</h3>

  <p>
    Account administrators create a separate API key per developer, team, or environment (in the Ollagraph Dashboard or via <code>POST /v1/keys</code>). Each key can carry a label, a project tag, and an optional daily credit cap, and usage can be reviewed in the dashboard.
  </p>

